{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>You shall surely rebuke your fellow, and you shall not bear sin on his account.</b> Specifically 'your fellow' in commandments — to exclude three classes who do not accept reproof, and they are: the wicked, fools, and scoffers. And your mnemonic is רְכִ״ל, as it is written (Proverbs 9:8): ״אַל תּוֹכַח לֵץ״, and it is written (ibid. 23:9): ״בְּאָזְנֵי כְסִיל אַל תְּדַבֵּר״, and it is written (ibid. 9:7): ״וּמוֹכִיחַ לְרָשָׁע מוּמוֹ״. For everyone who goes as a talebearer (רָכִיל) rejoices at another's calamity, and when he sees something shameful in his fellow he never rebukes him in order to turn him back from his way, but rather goes and spies on him in order to report it to the public and broadcast his disgrace, in the manner of all who go as talebearers. And these three classes despise reproof; therefore there is no obligation to rebuke them." "And since it says 'and you shall not bear sin on his account,' this implies that if you do not rebuke him then his sin will be borne upon you. And this is because all Israel are guarantors for one another. And one who lends to his fellow and gives him a guarantor — when the guarantor sees the borrower squandering his money, he rebukes him out of fear of the guarantee, lest he be required to pay on his account. So too, the mutual guarantee of all Israel is the cause of reproof; and if he does not accept it, then he is clear of it, for the strict line of the law does not require that one suffer on account of his fellow who is not under his authority. But in this matter alone Israel became guarantors: that when it is in his power to protest and he does not protest, then it is just that he be held liable on his account and bear his sin if he does not rebuke him. A parable was told about this: concerning one who was drilling a hole in a boat beneath himself — all the people of the boat cried out at him: What is this you are doing? He replied to them: Is it not beneath me that I am drilling? They said to him: If the water comes in beneath you, then the boat will sink entirely. Thus said Iyov to his friends: ״וְאַף אִם אָמְנָם שָׁגִיתִי אִתִּי תָּלִין מְשׁוּגָתִי״ (Job 19:4). And what did his friends answer him? ״כִּי יוֹסִיף עַל חַטָּאתוֹ פֶּשַׁע בֵּינֵינוּ יִסְפּוֹק״ (Job 34:37). Therefore it says: 'and you shall not bear sin on his account.'" "And adjacent to the commandment of reproof is the verse 'You shall not hate your brother in your heart.' For when love is found among Israel, each one desires his fellow's good, and therefore he rebukes him so that he not stumble in transgression. But when they hate one another, he never rebokes him — and on the contrary, he flatters him, saying: You have done righteously and have committed no offense. And all his intention is to draw evil upon him, because he rejoices in his downfall. And this trait — is it not the curse of the children of our nation from the day that the fist of baseless hatred gained strength, from the destruction of the Second Temple onward, and to this day it has not departed. And the plague goes and spreads further, until HaShem look down and see, and remove the heart of stone from our midst."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:43.864395537Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}